                           "Kansas Must Repeal SB 30 a licensing power grab across Kansas, thousands of hardworking professionals—from cosmetologists and barbers to plumbers and electricians—pay annual licensing fees out of their own pockets.They do not rely on a single cent of taxpayer money from the State General Fund.These professionals willingly pay their hard-earned money to support independent regulatory boards, trusting that their fees will fund specialized, expert representation and protect the integrity of their trade at the state Capitol. But a troubling shift in Topeka has turned this model on its head.With the passage of Senate Bill 30 (SB 30)—which was pushed through by Senate President Ty Masterson via a veto override of Governor Laura Kelly—the state legislature has stripped these independent boards of their autonomy.It is a system that takes the licensees\u0027 money, strips away their representation, and hands control over to politicians.For the sake of professional integrity and consumer safety, this overreach must be repealed.It strips away representation while keeping the cash.The core unfairness of SB 30 lies in its disruption of the fee-funded agency model.Under this law, state agencies and independent boards are entirely blocked from creating new occupational licenses or making any \"material changes\" to existing ones without explicit legislative ratification.When a cosmetologist or a barber pays their licensing fees, they pay specialized board members who understand the nuances, sanitation requirements, and evolving standards of their specific industry.SB 30 effectively tells these professionals, “We will keep taking your money to fund the state bureaucracy, but we are taking away your representation.”Instead of industry experts making regulatory decisions, those decisions are now subject to the whims of politicians who lack the technical expertise to judge what is best for the trade.The constant attack on our independent boards causes chaos and costs licensing more money, it also results in increased fees.This structural overreach does not happen in a vacuum; it creates immediate, tangible chaos for everyday workers.When politicians constantly attack and undermine the authority of independent boards, it forces these small agencies into a state of perpetual instability.Rather than focusing on smooth administrative operations, reciprocal licensing agreements with other states, or updating safety protocols, boards are forced to spend time and resources navigating complex legislative report filings and defending their existence in court.This manufactured chaos and bureaucratic red tape ultimately trickles downward. It disrupts the licensing pipeline, creates administrative backlogs, and ultimately costs the individual licensee more money in lost working hours and administrative friction.A Threat to Independent Boards. The legislative encroachment under SB 30 is part of a broader pattern targeting independent representation.Proposals like the forced, hostile merger of the Kansas Board of Barbering and the Kansas Board of Cosmetology highlight a continuous effort by legislative leadership to dilute the voting and regulatory power of independent trades.By consolidating and centralizing control, the legislature tilts the playing field in favor of corporate mega-chains over local, independent small business owners.What can we do about it???Repeal SB 30!! (Yes, we can do that!)Governor Laura Kelly stood with independent workers when she vetoed SB 30, rightly warning that inserting politicians into specialized licensing decisions adds nothing but \"bureaucratic red tape and bloated administrative busy work.\"Unfortunately, legislative leadership (Ty Masterson, Senate President) chose politics over professionals by forcing the bill into law anyway.Kansans who pay for their own professional representation deserve separate boards and independent advocates who look out for their industries. (Including KS Body Art!)It is time for lawmakers to respect the workers who fund these boards.Kansas must repeal the overreach of SB 30, return regulatory authority to industry experts, and restore the fair representation that licensees have rightfully paid for."
